[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 395,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 228,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58779,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 228,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intramuscular angioma is a proliferation of benign vascular channels within skeletal muscle, associated in most instances with variable amounts of mature adipose tissue.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 396,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 229,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58780,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 229,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9132/0 Intramuscular haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 397,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 230,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58781,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 230,
        "headingText": "<p>2E81.0Y &amp; XH0553 Other specified neoplastic haemangioma &amp; Intramuscular haemangioma</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-23T16:31:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 398,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 231,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58782,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> intramuscular haemangioma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> intramuscular-infiltrating angiolipoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58783,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58784,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intramuscular angioma most commonly affects the lower limbs, particularly the thigh and calf, followed by the head and neck, upper limbs, and trunk. Cases can present rarely in the mediastinum and retroperitoneum and exceptionally within cardiac muscle {18297465}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58785,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "<p>Typical presentation is of a slow-growing mass that is often painful, particularly after exercise. Pain is mainly present in tumours located in the limbs. Giant lesions may exceptionally induce osteolysis {22219438}. Radiological examination often reveals the presence of calcification due to phleboliths or metaplastic ossification. MRI is the most important radiological technique to establish the diagnosis {18362809}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T16:52:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58786,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although relatively uncommon, intramuscular angioma is one of the most frequent deep-seated soft tissue tumours. The age range is wide, but adolescents and young adults are most commonly affected (as many as 90% of cases) {5061701; 2013460; 5069204}, with equal sex distribution. Lesions have often been present for many years and it is therefore likely that many examples are congenital.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58787,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58788,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58789,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are often large and there is diffuse infiltration of the involved muscle. Variably sized vascular channels with thrombosis and haemorrhage are usually readily seen. The appearance of the tumour can be solid and yellowish as a result of the presence of adipose tissue. Lesions also appear solid when capillaries predominate.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58790,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "<p>Intramuscular angiomas are mostly composed of mixed vessel types, including lymphatics {5061701}, large thick-walled veins, a mixture of cavernous-like vascular spaces and capillaries, or a prominent arteriovenous component. Tumours purely composed of capillaries are more common in the head and neck area, and those with a predominant cavernous lymphatic component are seen mainly on the trunk, proximal upper limb, and head. Variable amounts of mature adipose tissue are almost always present and may be very prominent. This explains why intramuscular angioma was sometimes inappropriately known in the past as deep or infiltrating angiolipoma {4855281}. Atrophy of muscle fibres because of tumour infiltration often results in degenerative/reactive sarcolemmal changes with hyperchromatic nuclei. Perineural involvement may be present.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T16:52:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58791,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58792,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58793,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> mass composed of mixed vessel types including lymphatics; situated within muscle and often presents with pain (worse on exercise).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58794,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58795,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 270,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The rate of local recurrence is high (30&ndash;50%) and wide local excision is therefore recommended. Local recurrence seems to be determined only by size of the tumour and excision margins {17438470}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:48:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]